{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"289f7f1c16ae92bb","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a VPC CIDR block?**|What is a VPC CIDR block?]]","x":-1040,"y":0,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"385f2f5dd5132209","type":"text","text":"When you create a VPC, you choose an IP address range.  \nFor example:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThis range is called a `CIDR block`.  \nThe CIDR block decides what private IP addresses can exist inside your VPC.  ","x":-640,"y":0,"width":500,"height":234},
		{"id":"a44103ace8c1d955","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What does `10.0.0.0/16` mean?**|What does `10.0.0.0/16` mean?]]","x":-1040,"y":275,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"79b0077bff6b65df","type":"text","text":"```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThis means your VPC can use IP addresses from the `10.0.x.x` range.  \nFor example:\n```\n10.0.1.10\n10.0.2.25\n10.0.100.50\n```\nA `/16` CIDR block gives a large number of IP addresses.  ","x":-640,"y":275,"width":500,"height":330},
		{"id":"3b5bff92561533a2","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**Can you change the main VPC CIDR block later?**|Can you change the main VPC CIDR block later?]]","x":-1040,"y":720,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"a7b16c871e023967","type":"text","text":"After you create the VPC, the main CIDR block cannot be changed.  \nSo you should choose it carefully.  \nFor example, if you create your VPC with:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThat becomes the primary IP range for the VPC.  ","x":-640,"y":720,"width":500,"height":233},
		{"id":"d25399ac71f8a3a9","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What does CIDR block size mean?**|What does CIDR block size mean?]]","x":-1040,"y":-1480,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"9a79a0db405facf0","type":"text","text":"The CIDR block size controls how many IP addresses your VPC can have.  \nFor example:\n```\n/16 means 65,536 IP addresses\n/28 means 16 IP addresses\n```\nA smaller number after `/` means a bigger network.  \nA bigger number after `/` means a smaller network.  ","x":-640,"y":-1480,"width":500,"height":259},
		{"id":"1fa83391258a1004","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**Why should VPC CIDR blocks not overlap?**|Why should VPC CIDR blocks not overlap?]]","x":-1040,"y":-1175,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"f74258010eb3e1bb","type":"text","text":"Your VPC CIDR block should not overlap with other networks.  \nThis is very important when you connect your VPC to another network.  \nFor example:\n```\nCompany office network\nAWS VPC\nVPN network\nAnother VPC\n```\nIf the IP ranges overlap, routing becomes confusing.  \n**Example of overlapping CIDR problem**  \nImagine your company network uses:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThen your AWS VPC should not also use:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nBecause when traffic wants to go to:\n```\n10.0.1.20\n```\nThe system becomes confused.\n```\nIs 10.0.1.20 in the office network\\?**\n?\nOr is 10.0.1.20 inside the AWS VPC\\?**\n?\n```\nSo you should choose a different CIDR block for your VPC.  \nFor example:\n```\n10.10.0.0/16\n```\nor:\n```\n172.16.0.0/16\n```","x":-640,"y":-1175,"width":500,"height":1075},
		{"id":"19f62f4a35e15685","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a primary CIDR block?**|What is a primary CIDR block?]]","x":-1040,"y":1443,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"13001a153b10c9b0","type":"text","text":"A `primary CIDR block` is the main IP address range assigned to a VPC when it is created.  \nExample:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nThis is the main private IP range for the VPC.  ","x":-640,"y":1443,"width":500,"height":210},
		{"id":"9bd33dc8746989fc","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is a CIDR block?**|What is a CIDR block?]]","x":-1040,"y":1693,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"d3d1e7122563eb5f","type":"text","text":"A `CIDR block` is an IP address range.  \nIt can be used by:\n```\nVPC\nSubnet\nNetwork\n```\nExample:\n```\n10.0.0.0/16\n```\nor:\n```\n10.0.1.0/24\n```\n<!--SR:!2026-07-30,1,230-->","x":-640,"y":1693,"width":500,"height":460},
		{"id":"ff09b635271f561c","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is IPv4?**|What is IPv4?]]","x":-1040,"y":1013,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"62c28db73d034db5","type":"text","text":"`IPv4` is the older and most common IP address system.  \nExample IPv4 address:\n```\n192.168.1.1\n```\nMost common private network ranges use IPv4.  ","x":-640,"y":1013,"width":500,"height":190},
		{"id":"cbf0b060081cdced","type":"text","text":"[[2. Aws/1. Aws fcaj/Explore AWS Services/5. Networking Essentials with Amazon Virtual Private Cloud (VPC)/1. In troduction/6. Checklist/1. Checklist for Amazon VPC#**What is IPv6?**|What is IPv6?]]","x":-1040,"y":1263,"width":350,"height":80},
		{"id":"b49368ab5b9a2e49","type":"text","text":"`IPv6` is a newer IP address system with a much larger address space.  \nIPv6 addresses are longer than IPv4 addresses.  ","x":-640,"y":1263,"width":500,"height":120}
	],
	"edges":[
